{
	title: 'Use one shared chrome implementation for every web card',
	spec: 'Every web transcript card uses CardHeader for timestamp, reference, actions, expansion affordance and sticky chrome. Timestamp starts align on mobile and desktop; cards contain no disclosure triangles or reserved triangle indentation. Cover prompts, replies, thinking, tools, background results, prompt-file changes, queued and optimistic messages, commands, notices, dividers and active or completed questions. Kind-specific code supplies content and metadata, never another header or timestamp button. Thinking keeps its content-first inline layout: timestamp and metadata stick without pinning the whole reasoning body. Every other card header sticks within its card. This replaces the tool/background-only sticky rule and the headerless change-card rule in ar. Terminal rendering stays unchanged. Preserve grouped change summaries and time ranges, colors, previews, streaming, fold state and native forms. Card expansion has one event policy; card, command and raw-output controls share Disclosure while nested controls keep independent state. References share CardReference. Preserve keyboard access, names, aria-expanded, touch targets, links, selection and reader-owned scroll. Remove superseded markup and CSS. Keep one compact browser matrix for one shared header per card, aligned timestamps, triangle absence, no horizontal overflow and actual sticky positions at mobile and desktop widths, in short and long states. Existing interaction checks cover controls, links, forms and selection. Add no per-card timers, duplicate observers or history scans. Structural body predicates are memoized so streaming updates preserve Markdown nodes and native selection; shared header labels resolve once.',
